### How does TreeMap maintain sorted keys?

TreeMap uses a Red-Black tree internally. Every node stores a key-value pair, a reference to left and right children, a parent reference, and a color (red or black). The tree maintains the invariant that all keys in the left subtree are less than the node's key, and all keys in the right subtree are greater.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class TreeMapSortedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // String implements Comparable → natural alphabetical order
        TreeMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
        accounts.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        accounts.put("FD4001", new BigDecimal("500000"));
        accounts.put("LON5001", new BigDecimal("200000"));

        // Iteration: always sorted by key
        System.out.println("Sorted accounts:");
        accounts.forEach((id, balance) ->
            System.out.println("  " + id + ": " + balance));

        // NavigableMap operations
        System.out.println("\nFirst: " + accounts.firstKey());
        System.out.println("Last: " + accounts.lastKey());
        System.out.println("Before 'G': " + accounts.lowerKey("G"));
        System.out.println("After 'G': " + accounts.higherKey("G"));
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted accounts:
  CUR3001: 75000
  FD4001: 500000
  LON5001: 200000
  SAV2001: 150000

First: CUR3001
Last: SAV2001
Before 'G': FD4001
After 'G': LON5001
```

### What happens without Comparable or Comparator?

If you use a custom class that does not implement Comparable and do not provide a Comparator, TreeMap throws `ClassCastException` on the second `put()` call. The first put succeeds because there is nothing to compare with (it becomes the root).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ClassCastExceptionDemo {
    // Does NOT implement Comparable
    static class Account {
        String id;
        Account(String id) { this.id = id; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeMap<Account, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

        // First put: succeeds (nothing to compare with)
        map.put(new Account("SAV2001"), "Rajesh");
        System.out.println("First put succeeded");

        // Second put: ClassCastException!
        try {
            map.put(new Account("CUR3001"), "Priya");
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Second put: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // FIX: provide a Comparator
        TreeMap<Account, String> fixed = new TreeMap<>(
            Comparator.comparing(a -> a.id)
        );
        fixed.put(new Account("SAV2001"), "Rajesh");
        fixed.put(new Account("CUR3001"), "Priya");
        System.out.println("Fixed: " + fixed.size() + " entries");
    }
}
```

```text
First put succeeded
Second put: class Account cannot be cast to class java.lang.Comparable
Fixed: 2 entries
```

### How do you use TreeMap with custom objects?

Two approaches: implement Comparable in the class, or pass a Comparator to TreeMap.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

// Approach 1: Implement Comparable
record Trade(String tradeId, java.time.LocalDate date, BigDecimal amount)
    implements Comparable<Trade> {

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Trade other) {
        return this.date.compareTo(other.date); // sort by date
    }
}

public class TreeMapCustomKeyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Approach 1: Comparable key
        TreeMap<Trade, String> byDate = new TreeMap<>();
        byDate.put(new Trade("T1", java.time.LocalDate.of(2024, 3, 15), new BigDecimal("50000")), "Executed");
        byDate.put(new Trade("T2", java.time.LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 10), new BigDecimal("75000")), "Settled");
        System.out.println("By date: " + byDate.keySet());

        // Approach 2: Comparator
        TreeMap<Trade, String> byAmount = new TreeMap<>(
            Comparator.comparing(Trade::amount).reversed()
        );
        byAmount.putAll(byDate);
        System.out.println("By amount desc: " + byAmount.keySet());
    }
}
```

```text
By date: [Trade[tradeId=T2, date=2024-01-10, amount=75000], Trade[tradeId=T1, date=2024-03-15, amount=50000]]
By amount desc: [Trade[tradeId=T2, date=2024-01-10, amount=75000], Trade[tradeId=T1, date=2024-03-15, amount=50000]]
```

### What are the NavigableMap range operations?

| Method | Returns |
|---|---|
| firstKey() / firstEntry() | Smallest key/entry |
| lastKey() / lastEntry() | Largest key/entry |
| lowerKey(k) | Greatest key strictly < k |
| floorKey(k) | Greatest key <= k |
| ceilingKey(k) | Smallest key >= k |
| higherKey(k) | Smallest key strictly > k |
| subMap(from, to) | View of keys in [from, to) |
| headMap(to) | View of keys < to |
| tailMap(from) | View of keys >= from |
| descendingMap() | Reverse-order view |
| pollFirstEntry() | Remove and return smallest |
| pollLastEntry() | Remove and return largest |

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Inconsistent compareTo and equals

```java
// TreeMap uses compareTo for equality, not equals!
// If a.compareTo(b) == 0 but !a.equals(b):
// TreeMap treats them as the SAME key (overwrite)
// HashMap treats them as DIFFERENT keys
// Always keep compareTo consistent with equals
```

#### Mistake 2: Forgetting that subMap returns a view

```java
TreeMap<String, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
map.put("A", "1"); map.put("B", "2"); map.put("C", "3");

var sub = map.subMap("A", "C"); // view: {A=1, B=2}
sub.put("A2", "1.5"); // modifies the ORIGINAL map!
System.out.println(map); // {A=1, A2=1.5, B=2, C=3}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Red-Black tree guarantees.** The tree is always balanced within a factor of 2: the longest path from root to leaf is at most twice the shortest. This guarantees O(log n) worst case, unlike a BST which can degrade to O(n).

- **ConcurrentSkipListMap.** The thread-safe equivalent of TreeMap. Uses a skip list (probabilistic balancing) instead of a Red-Black tree. Supports NavigableMap and ConcurrentMap.

- **compareTo must be total.** The Comparator or Comparable must define a total ordering: every pair of elements must be comparable. Returning 0 only for truly equal elements.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the internal data structure of TreeMap?" – Red-Black tree.
- "What happens if the key class does not implement Comparable?" – ClassCastException on the second put.
- "What is the time complexity of TreeMap.get?" – O(log n).
- "Does TreeMap allow null keys?" – No (compareTo throws NPE).
